{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1625 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "65b251932a85b2e768a675fbe127e4fa634033dcb7f9f2138a9aa0257a68c108" :edb/db-before-t 1624 :edb/db-after-t 1625 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534937 50 #inst "2026-10-08T11:30:40.045Z" 13194139534937 true] [17592186124184 1100 "Given that $f(x,y)=\\dfrac yx$, find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ exists." 13194139534937 false] [17592186124184 1100 "Given that $f(x,y)=\\dfrac yx,$ find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ exists." 13194139534937 true] [17592186124184 1145 "Remember that the partial derivative of a function exists where the original function is defined *and* the partial derivative is defined.\n\nFirst, notice that $f(x,y)$ is not defined when $x=0$. So, the domain of $f$ is\n\n$$\nD_f = \\{(x,y) \\,: \\, x\\ne 0 \\}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{y}(x, y) &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe partial derivative $f_y(x,y)$ is also not defined for $x=0$. So, the domain of $f_y$ is\n\n$$\nD_{f_y} = \\{(x,y) \\,: \\, x\\ne 0 \\}\n$$\n\nTo compute the set where\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\n$$\n\nexists, we take the intersection of the domain of the original function $(D_f)$ with the domain of the partial derivative $(D_{f_y})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{f}∩D_{f_{y}} &= {(x, y): x \\ne 0}∩{(x, y): x \\ne 0} \\\\\n&= {(x, y): x \\ne 0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\n$$\n\nexists on the set\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\,: \\, x \\neq 0 \\}\n$$" 13194139534937 false] [17592186124184 1145 "Remember that the partial derivative of a function exists where the original function is defined *and* the partial derivative is defined. \n\nFirst, notice that $f(x,y)$ is not defined when $x=0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\nD_f = \\{(x,y) \\, : \\, x\\ne 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe partial derivative $f_y(x,y)$ is also not defined for $x=0.$ So, the domain of $f_y$ is\n\n$$\nD_{f_y} = \\{(x,y) \\, : \\, x\\ne 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the set where  $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ exists, we take the intersection of the domain of the original function $(D_f)$ with the domain of the partial derivative $(D_{f_y})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ exists on the set $\\{ (x,y)\\, : \\, x \\neq 0 \\}.$" 13194139534937 true] [17592186124185 1100 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x,y,z)=xz\\ln{(x+y+z)}$?\n\n1. $f_x(-1,-1,-1)$ is defined\n2. $f_y(1,0,-1)$ is defined\n3. $f_z(1,1,1)$ is defined" 13194139534937 false] [17592186124185 1100 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x,y,z)=xz\\ln{(x+y+z)}?$\n\n- $f_x(-1,-1,-1)$ is defined\n\n- $f_y(1,0,-1)$ is defined\n\n- $f_z(1,1,1)$ is defined" 13194139534937 true] [17592186124185 1145 "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &= z\\ln (x + y + z) + \\frac{xz}{x + y + z} \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y} &= \\frac{xz}{x + y + z} \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z} &= x\\ln (x + y + z) + \\frac{xz}{x + y + z}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(-1,-1,-1)$ into $f$, we get\n$f(-1,-1,-1) = (-1)(-1)\\ln{(-1-1-1)} = \\ln{(-3)}$,\nwhich is undefined since the logarithm exists only for positive arguments. Therefore, $f_x$ cannot exist at this point.\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0,-1)$ into $f_y$, we get\n$f(1,0,-1) = \\dfrac{(1)(-1)}{1+0-1} = \\dfrac{-1}{0}$,\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,1,1)$:\n$f(1,1,1)= (1)(1)\\ln(1+1+1)=\\ln(3)$\nLikewise $f_z$ is also defined at $(1,1,1)$:\n$f_{z}(1, 1, 1)|= (1)\\ln (1 + 1 + 1) + ((1)(1))/(1 + 1 + 1) = \\ln (3) + \\frac{1}{3}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139534937 false] [17592186124185 1145 "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+\\frac{xz}{x+y+z} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{xz}{x+y+z} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+\\frac{xz}{x+y+z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(-1,-1,-1)$ into $f,$ we get\n\n$$\nf(-1,-1,-1) = (-1)(-1)\\ln{(-1-1-1)} = \\ln{(-3)},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since the logarithm exists only for positive arguments. Therefore, $f_x$ cannot exist at this point.\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0,-1)$ into $f_y,$ we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0,-1) = \\dfrac{(1)(-1)}{1+0-1} = \\dfrac{-1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf(1,1,1)= (1)(1)\\ln(1+1+1)=\\ln(3)\n$$\n\nLikewise $f_z$ is also defined at $(1,1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{z}(1,1,1) & =(1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+1+1)+\\frac{(1)(1)}{1+1+1}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139534937 true] [17592186124284 1100 "Given that $f(x,y)= \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y^2}\\right)$ and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = -\\dfrac{2}{y},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ on which all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." 13194139534937 true] [17592186124284 1100 "Given that $f(x,y)= \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y^2}\\right)$ and\n$\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = -\\dfrac{2}{y}$,\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ on which all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." 13194139534937 false] [17592186124284 1145 "Remember that the partial derivatives of a function exist where the original function is defined and the partial derivatives are defined.\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y^2}\\right)\n$$\n\nis defined only when\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{y^2}>0\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is\n\n$$\nD_f = \\{(x,y) \\,: \\, x > 0, \\: y \\neq 0 \\}\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following:\n\n- The partial derivative $f_x(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is defined only when $x \\neq 0$. So, the domain of $f_x$ is\n$D_{f_x} = \\{(x,y) \\,: \\, x \\neq 0 \\}$.\n- The partial derivative $f_y(x,y) = -\\dfrac{2}{y}$ is defined only when $y \\neq 0$. So, the domain of $f_y$ is\n$D_{f_y} = \\{(x,y) \\,: \\, y \\neq 0 \\}$.\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{f}∩D_{f_{x}}∩D_{f_{y}} &= {(x, y): x > 0, y \\ne 0}∩{(x, y): x \\ne 0}∩{(x, y): y \\ne 0} \\\\\n&= {(x, y): x > 0, y \\ne 0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534937 false] [17592186124284 1145 "Remember that the partial derivatives of a function exist where the original function is defined and the partial derivatives are defined.\n\nFirst, notice that $f(x,y) = \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y^2}\\right)$ is defined only when $\\dfrac{x}{y^2}>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\nD_f = \\{(x,y) \\, : \\, x > 0, \\: y \\neq 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following:\n\n- The partial derivative $f_x(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is defined only when $x \\neq 0.$ So, the domain of $f_x$ is \n\n$$\nD_{f_x} = \\{(x,y) \\, : \\, x \\neq 0 \\}.\n$$\n\n- The partial derivative $f_y(x,y) =  -\\dfrac{2}{y}$ is defined only when $y \\neq 0.$ So, the domain of $f_y$ is \n\n$$\nD_{f_y} = \\{(x,y) \\, : \\, y \\neq 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y\\ne 0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534937 true] [17592186124383 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321034 13194139534937 true] [17592186124383 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321041 13194139534937 true] [17592186124383 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321076 13194139534937 true] [17592186124383 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321083 13194139534937 true] [17592186124383 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321090 13194139534937 true] [17592186124384 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321062 13194139534937 true] [17592186124384 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321069 13194139534937 true] [17592186124384 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321111 13194139534937 true] [17592186124478 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321048 13194139534937 true] [17592186124478 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321055 13194139534937 true] [17592186124478 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321097 13194139534937 true] [17592186124478 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321104 13194139534937 true] [17592186124478 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321118 13194139534937 true] [17592186124911 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534937 true] [17592186124911 1100 "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x, y) = \\ln (\\frac{x}{x + y})$?\n\n1. Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1, 1)$\n2. $f_{x}(0, 1)$ is defined\n3. $f_{y}(0, 1)$ is defined" 13194139534937 false] [17592186124911 1100 "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{x+y})?$\n\n- Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,1)$\n- ${f}_{x}(0,1)$ is defined\n- ${f}_{y}(0,1)$ is defined" 13194139534937 true] [17592186124911 1145 "First, we note that $f$ is defined only when\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{x+y}>0.\n$$\n\nAt points in the domain of $f,$ we can compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1\\cdot (x+y)-x\\cdot 1}{(x+y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{y}{(x+y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x(x+y)} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{x}\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{(x+y)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is defined at $(1,1),$ since\n\n$$\nf(1,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{1+1})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}),\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are defined there:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =\\frac{1}{1(1+1)}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,1) & =-\\frac{1}{1+1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. At $(0,1),$\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{x+y}=\\frac{0}{0+1}=0,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\nf(0,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0),\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined. Therefore, ${f}_{x}(0,1)$ is not defined.\n\n- Statement III is false for the same reason. Since $f$ is not defined at $(0,1),$ the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(0,1)$ is not defined. The simplified expression $-\\frac{1}{x+y}$ cannot be used to define the partial derivative at a point outside the domain of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534937 true] [17592186124916 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534937 true] [17592186124916 1100 "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x, y, z) = \\frac{z^{2}}{x^{2} - y^{2}}$?\n\n1. The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $(1,-1, 0)$\n2. $f_{x}(0, 1, 1)$ is defined\n3. $f_{z}(1, 1, 0)$ is defined" 13194139534937 false] [17592186124916 1100 "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}?$\n\n- The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $(1,-1,0)$\n- ${f}_{x}(0,1,1)$ is defined\n- ${f}_{z}(1,1,0)$ is defined" 13194139534937 true] [17592186124916 1145 "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})^{2}}\\cdot (2x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x{z}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})^{2}}\\cdot (-2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y{z}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{2z}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(1,-1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,-1,0)=\\frac{(0)^{2}}{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(0,1,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,1,1)=\\frac{(1)^{2}}{(0)^{2}-(1)^{2}}=-1,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(0,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1,1) & =-\\frac{2(0)(1)}{(0)^{2}-(1)^{2}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(1,1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,1,0)=\\frac{(0)^{2}}{(1)^{2}-(1)^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'." 13194139534937 true] [17592186321034 1017 #edb/ref 17592186321035 13194139534937 true] [17592186321034 1089 "q-132643" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321034 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534937 true] [17592186321034 1100 "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{x-y}},$ find the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ exists." 13194139534937 true] [17592186321034 1113 #uuid "20a0786c-29f2-5827-9394-c6b48bd4e191" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321034 1145 "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{x-y}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{2\\sqrt{(x-y)^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321035 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321036 13194139534937 true] [17592186321035 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321037 13194139534937 true] [17592186321035 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321038 13194139534937 true] [17592186321035 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321039 13194139534937 true] [17592186321035 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321040 13194139534937 true] [17592186321035 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534937 true] [17592186321035 1041 "selection" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321035 1104 #edb/ref 17592186321039 13194139534937 true] [17592186321035 1110 #uuid "c4044f33-0f97-50e9-8796-35b8aa64c4c0" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321036 1034 "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z>0}" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321036 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534937 true] [17592186321036 1111 #uuid "4f807010-9735-5b5f-868d-a3eadaec52de" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321037 1034 "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-z\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,z>0}" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321037 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534937 true] [17592186321037 1111 #uuid "cb29efab-50a2-577e-bf75-d97d809f475c" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321038 1034 "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y\\ne 0}" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321038 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534937 true] [17592186321038 1111 #uuid "d7db425f-92aa-5505-8595-79eaffae9552" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321039 1034 "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0}" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321039 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534937 true] [17592186321039 1111 #uuid "a0223643-4d06-5c12-a9ce-b08f40b8da8b" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321040 1034 "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-z>0\\,\\text{and}\\,z\\ne 0}" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321040 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534937 true] [17592186321040 1111 #uuid "0f6f8f70-7d2f-5b5c-b14d-7803ceb15330" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321041 1017 #edb/ref 17592186321042 13194139534937 true] [17592186321041 1089 "q-132645" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321041 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534937 true] [17592186321041 1100 "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)}{y},$ find the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}$ exists." 13194139534937 true] [17592186321041 1113 #uuid "27098d48-7989-5b6b-ace6-3cb96c67f741" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321041 1145 "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+z & >0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+z} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y(x+z)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{z}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+z & \\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{z}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{z}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z\\ne 0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $z$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z\\ne 0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y\\ne 0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321042 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321043 13194139534937 true] [17592186321042 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321044 13194139534937 true] [17592186321042 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321045 13194139534937 true] [17592186321042 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321046 13194139534937 true] [17592186321042 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321047 13194139534937 true] [17592186321042 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534937 true] [17592186321042 1041 "selection" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321042 1104 #edb/ref 17592186321044 13194139534937 true] [17592186321042 1110 #uuid "e6891fc5-4b5c-5983-8d8a-1601638739d1" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321043 1034 "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z\\ne 0}" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321043 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534937 true] [17592186321043 1111 #uuid "cbb93424-5074-50a9-abcb-e67974095a44" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321044 1034 "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y\\ne 0}" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321044 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534937 true] [17592186321044 1111 #uuid "fe1f3b1f-aa2d-5fbc-80dd-0a20104eded1" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321045 1034 "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z\\ne 0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y>0}" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321045 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534937 true] [17592186321045 1111 #uuid "a42f3225-0102-58da-b3bf-2e1ad5e4bb14" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321046 1034 "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y>0}" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321046 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534937 true] [17592186321046 1111 #uuid "df99b2ec-312b-5d55-bcd5-f93f3eaffe36" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321047 1034 "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z>0}" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321047 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534937 true] [17592186321047 1111 #uuid "16ca7188-21df-5171-ba37-e3b6a73e90c5" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321048 1017 #edb/ref 17592186321049 13194139534937 true] [17592186321048 1089 "q-132650" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321048 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534937 true] [17592186321048 1100 "Given that $g(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}{z}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)},\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{2y}{z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)},\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂z}=-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}{{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ where all partial derivatives of $g$ exist." 13194139534937 true] [17592186321048 1113 #uuid "1579cb82-f401-5fee-aadc-be6820a852e8" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321048 1145 "First, notice that $g(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}{z}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1>0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y,z)=\\frac{2x}{z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1\\ne 0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1,\\,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domain of ${g}_{y}(x,y,z)$ is the same: \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}={D}_{{g}_{x}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1,\\,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${g}_{z}(x,y,z)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}{{z}^{2}}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1>0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{z}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{z}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{z}} & ={D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{z}} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1,\\,z\\ne 0} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321049 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321050 13194139534937 true] [17592186321049 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321051 13194139534937 true] [17592186321049 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321052 13194139534937 true] [17592186321049 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321053 13194139534937 true] [17592186321049 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321054 13194139534937 true] [17592186321049 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534937 true] [17592186321049 1041 "selection" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321049 1104 #edb/ref 17592186321050 13194139534937 true] [17592186321049 1110 #uuid "ca829a24-7567-5ce3-8954-df7154fedce3" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321050 1034 "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0}" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321050 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534937 true] [17592186321050 1111 #uuid "ebd16056-188b-5412-b1c9-b33a5b3b32ea" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321051 1034 "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1,\\,z\\ne 0}" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321051 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534937 true] [17592186321051 1111 #uuid "7702e375-c362-5b2a-915f-b22332338a2e" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321052 1034 "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1}" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321052 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534937 true] [17592186321052 1111 #uuid "0fa92c70-ae91-5c73-b1cd-b9c1b0396a39" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321053 1034 "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1}" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321053 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534937 true] [17592186321053 1111 #uuid "d0fa6ca5-8fd5-5f3c-be48-4fe1ad398997" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321054 1034 "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0,\\,z>0}" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321054 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534937 true] [17592186321054 1111 #uuid "845b9aa7-8ebe-5fab-b45c-1503b466a436" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321055 1017 #edb/ref 17592186321056 13194139534937 true] [17592186321055 1089 "q-132651" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321055 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534937 true] [17592186321055 1100 "Given that $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{y}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{2y\\sqrt{x}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ where all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." 13194139534937 true] [17592186321055 1113 #uuid "ebb24a0e-97b7-5573-87dd-3d7664af2b3e" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321055 1145 "First, notice that $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{y}$ is defined only when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2y\\sqrt{x}}$ is defined only when $x>0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${f}_{y}(x,y)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}$ is defined only when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321056 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321057 13194139534937 true] [17592186321056 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321058 13194139534937 true] [17592186321056 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321059 13194139534937 true] [17592186321056 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321060 13194139534937 true] [17592186321056 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321061 13194139534937 true] [17592186321056 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534937 true] [17592186321056 1041 "selection" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321056 1104 #edb/ref 17592186321061 13194139534937 true] [17592186321056 1110 #uuid "1aeb9b2f-c57d-585e-8b91-7b853b9a6e87" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321057 1034 "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ne 0}" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321057 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534937 true] [17592186321057 1111 #uuid "2ef044d8-00e4-5a86-97b5-193928d27dc8" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321058 1034 "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y>0}" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321058 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534937 true] [17592186321058 1111 #uuid "58bcff5a-e634-500e-bc89-63a21964363f" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321059 1034 "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y<0}" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321059 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534937 true] [17592186321059 1111 #uuid "31da173d-c83c-5760-8198-7a58e9e2bd59" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321060 1034 "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x<0,\\,y\\ne 0}" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321060 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534937 true] [17592186321060 1111 #uuid "e1f01b9a-228d-5e66-ac6a-646a3cef7a1e" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321061 1034 "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y\\ne 0}" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321061 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534937 true] [17592186321061 1111 #uuid "c3df19f4-b5e0-5c0e-a8fa-e953ef42c0d6" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321062 1017 #edb/ref 17592186321063 13194139534937 true] [17592186321062 1089 "q-132675" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321062 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534937 true] [17592186321062 1100 "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)?$\n\n- The partial derivative with respect to $y$ is defined at $(-1,-1,0)$\n- ${f}_{x}(0,1,1)$ is defined\n- ${f}_{z}(1,-1,1)$ is defined" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321062 1113 #uuid "5934557b-111f-5bfc-a8d6-ea9621318b4e" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321062 1145 "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) \\\\  & =z\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{x} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) \\\\  & =z\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{y} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}(z) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(-1,-1,0)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(-1,-1,0)=0\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡((-1)\\cdot (-1))=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(-1,-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-1,-1,0) & =\\frac{0}{(-1)}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(0,1,1)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,1,1)=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\n- Statement III is also false. If we substitute $(1,-1,1)$ into ${f}_{z}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{z}(1,-1,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\cdot (-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1),\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139534937 true] [17592186321063 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321064 13194139534937 true] [17592186321063 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321065 13194139534937 true] [17592186321063 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321066 13194139534937 true] [17592186321063 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321067 13194139534937 true] [17592186321063 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321068 13194139534937 true] [17592186321063 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534937 true] [17592186321063 1041 "selection" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321063 1104 #edb/ref 17592186321065 13194139534937 true] [17592186321063 1110 #uuid "0de7b80b-5f05-59c1-a618-c8892b6e34a1" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321064 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321064 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534937 true] [17592186321064 1111 #uuid "50760f06-568a-554b-974b-998aa4496d42" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321065 1034 "I only" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321065 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534937 true] [17592186321065 1111 #uuid "fcec31f0-e6aa-5123-9ae9-298ae226d18c" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321066 1034 "III only" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321066 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534937 true] [17592186321066 1111 #uuid "53429129-c47b-5edb-8721-1e4c9a35b804" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321067 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321067 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534937 true] [17592186321067 1111 #uuid "e22a56cc-af57-5688-9905-3a73885bc07e" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321068 1034 "II only" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321068 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534937 true] [17592186321068 1111 #uuid "0fa85b43-88d9-5ca4-af25-92ff251fb0e0" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321069 1017 #edb/ref 17592186321070 13194139534937 true] [17592186321069 1089 "q-132728" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321069 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534937 true] [17592186321069 1100 "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2)?$\n\n- Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(0,2)$\n- ${f}_{x}(1,0)$ is defined\n- ${f}_{y}(1,1)$ is defined" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321069 1113 #uuid "c8cb7726-770d-51b1-bf05-a04aa01afc5b" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321069 1145 "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(0,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,2) & =\\frac{2(0)}{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2}=0 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,2) & =\\frac{2(2)}{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2}+{0}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1)\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$ Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is also false. If we substitute $(1,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2}+{1}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0)\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$ Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139534937 true] [17592186321070 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321071 13194139534937 true] [17592186321070 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321072 13194139534937 true] [17592186321070 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321073 13194139534937 true] [17592186321070 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321074 13194139534937 true] [17592186321070 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321075 13194139534937 true] [17592186321070 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534937 true] [17592186321070 1041 "selection" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321070 1104 #edb/ref 17592186321073 13194139534937 true] [17592186321070 1110 #uuid "a7797787-569b-5b73-b30f-a9b874e681b9" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321071 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321071 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534937 true] [17592186321071 1111 #uuid "d39d75d2-2db6-596b-8542-38cb5d084810" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321072 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321072 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534937 true] [17592186321072 1111 #uuid "3343b8b3-61fa-59dc-b89f-11053150ab75" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321073 1034 "I only" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321073 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534937 true] [17592186321073 1111 #uuid "28263756-4556-55be-bba1-c952dc89cb87" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321074 1034 "III only" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321074 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534937 true] [17592186321074 1111 #uuid "8f1671e8-25ed-5742-884f-083d45a15ed9" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321075 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321075 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534937 true] [17592186321075 1111 #uuid "97674ece-fd1a-5cf1-83c9-b385e72ed6ce" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321076 1017 #edb/ref 17592186321077 13194139534937 true] [17592186321076 1089 "q-36624" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321076 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534937 true] [17592186321076 1100 "Given that $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}),$ find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ exists." 13194139534937 true] [17592186321076 1113 #uuid "18ddac77-0ded-55cf-96b4-1d32b4183800" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321076 1145 "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2} & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}>0}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2} & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534937 true] [17592186321077 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321078 13194139534937 true] [17592186321077 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321079 13194139534937 true] [17592186321077 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321080 13194139534937 true] [17592186321077 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321081 13194139534937 true] [17592186321077 1000 